\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

\paragraph{Practitioner decision framework.}
\looseness=-1 Three zero-cost diagnostics predict self-verification failure before deployment. $K_\text{eff} \leq 1.3$: symbolic aggregation collapses to majority voting ($\Delta = -0.98$\,pp). SC $< 40\%$: constraints amplify errors ($\Delta = -2.83$\,pp). $C_0 < 5\%$: near-unanimous traces render aggregation moot. Only the intermediate regime (SC $\sim$50--60\%, $K_\text{eff} > 1.8$) offers marginal gain; practitioners should compute these before investing in symbolic reasoning.

\paragraph{SCB beyond SICA.}
\looseness=-1 SCB extends to any method extracting structured representations from a model's own outputs---self-verification \citep{huang2024selfcorrect}, self-refinement \citep{madaan2023selfrefine}, and chain-of-verification \citep{dhuliawala2023chainofverification} all face the same distributional prior. Our debate control ($\kappa = 0.15$, $-18.6$\,pp) confirms that independence and quality are at odds under same-model constraints.

\paragraph{Design principles for escaping SCB.}
\looseness=-1 The cross-architecture escape (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}) identifies three necessary conditions: (i)~\emph{structural independence}---no shared architecture or training data; (ii)~\emph{positive capability gap}---the verifier's adjusted contribution exceeds the generator's SC; (iii)~\emph{task-verifier alignment}---the verification paradigm matches task structure. These explain why 27 same-model interventions fail ($\kappa \geq 0.40$) while a 406M encoder succeeds ($+11.00$\,pp). Trained extractors capturing $>$12\% of the oracle ceiling ($+29.90$\,pp) are the most promising direction (Appendix~\ref{app:extended-discussion}). Formal self-verification creates a \emph{symbolic mirage}---provably sound machinery adding no information beyond majority voting---and the path forward lies in structurally independent verification, guided by $K_\text{eff}$, BR, and $C_0$.

\section{Cross-Architecture Proof-of-Concept}
\label{sec:solutions}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

\paragraph{Method.}
\looseness=-1 We test with DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli}, classifying (premise, conclusion) pairs as entailment, contradiction, or neutral. This proof-of-concept is limited to propositional-logic benchmarks whose premise-conclusion structure naturally suits NLI; generalization to non-logical domains remains untested. In combo mode, each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times alongside SC's $K$ votes. We evaluate on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$) and ProofWriter depth-5 ($n = 600$).

\paragraph{Main result.}
\looseness=-1 DeBERTa NLI alone achieves 49.51\% on FOLIO (below SC's 55.88\%) and 55.00\% on ProofWriter (above SC's 39.33\%). On FOLIO ($n = 204$), combo $w{=}3$ yields $+2.94$\,pp (n.s.). On ProofWriter ($n = 600$), $w{=}3$ achieves $+6.67$\,pp ($p < 0.001$) and $w{=}5$ reaches $+11.00$\,pp ($p < 0.001$)---while same-model SICA \emph{degrades} ProofWriter ($-2.83$\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), illustrating that corrective signal must originate outside the model's distribution (\S\ref{sec:mirage}). After BH correction ($m{=}12$), only Mistral-7B ProofWriter conditions ($w{=}3$, $w{=}5$) remain significant.

\paragraph{Why it works.}
\looseness=-1 Three conditions jointly enable the escape---structural independence, positive capability gap, and task-verifier alignment---expanded as design principles in \S\ref{sec:discussion}. Per-label analysis confirms NLI and SC excel on complementary labels (Appendix~\ref{app:multi-nli}); all three NLI architectures produce gains at $w \geq 3$.

\paragraph{Capability gap hypothesis.}
\looseness=-1 Four generators spanning a 46-point SC range on ProofWriter ($n{=}600$), all paired with DeBERTa-large-mnli (55.00\% standalone; Table~\ref{tab:cross_generator_nli}): only Mistral-7B (SC 39.33\%, below the verifier) gains significantly ($+6.67$\,pp, $p < 0.001$); stronger generators show no improvement. The monotonic relationship holds across three NLI architectures and six verifier-generator pairs (Spearman $\rho = 1.0$, $p = 0.003$, $n{=}6$; Figure~\ref{fig:capability-gap}, Appendix~\ref{app:capability-gap}). A $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix confirms BR $> 1$ in all eight cells ($1.94$--$2.70$; Appendix~\ref{app:2x2}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-generator NLI escape with DeBERTa-large-mnli verifier (55.00\% standalone, ProofWriter $n{=}600$). Combo gain decreases monotonically with generator capability ($w{=}3$).}
\label{tab:cross_generator_nli}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
Generator & Family & SC & Gap & Combo $\Delta$ ($w{=}3$) & $p$ \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & Mistral & 39.33\% & $+$15.67 & $+$6.67\,pp & $<$0.001 \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & LLaMA & 72.50\% & $-$17.50 & $+$0.83\,pp & 0.66 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & Qwen & 74.00\% & $-$19.00 & $+$0.67\,pp & 0.50 \\
Qwen3-14B & Qwen & 85.83\% & $-$30.83 & $+$0.50\,pp & 0.45 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Disagreement-Routed Cascade.}
\looseness=-1 DRC invokes NLI only below consensus threshold $\tau$, recovering 90\% of full-combo gain at ${\sim}$35\% NLI cost ($\tau{=}0.7$; Table~\ref{tab:drc}). Gains concentrate on weak generators (SC $< 40\%$), consistent with the capability gap. Regime-dependent bottleneck analysis appears in Appendix~\ref{app:2x2}.

\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\looseness=-1 We test the three predictions of the symbolic mirage hypothesis (\S\ref{sec:mirage}).

\subsection{Condorcet Effective-K}
\label{sec:effective-k}

\looseness=-1 Table~\ref{tab:independence} (Appendix) reports Fleiss' $\kappa$ and Effective-K (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}) across 15 conditions at $K = 12$. Among instruction-tuned models, $\kappa$ ranges from 0.21 to 0.82, yielding $K_{\text{eff}} = 1.20\text{--}3.62$ as an ordinal diagnostic: higher $K_{\text{eff}}$ indicates greater vote diversity, though it should not be interpreted as a literal effective sample size (\S\ref{sec:condorcet}). Across four Mistral-7B seeds, $\kappa \in [0.41, 0.51]$ yet $\Delta$ ranges from $+3.43$ to $-0.49$\,pp, indicating seed-level constraint quality matters beyond independence. Positive $\Delta$ requires both sufficient $K_{\text{eff}}$ ($\leq 1.30$ never improves) and SC accuracy ($K_{\text{eff}} = 3.62$ with SC $= 39\%$ degrades). All four model families produce $\kappa \geq 0.41$ (Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}).


\subsection{Symbolic Confirmation Bias}
\label{sec:confirmation-bias}

\looseness=-1 Self-extracted constraints systematically align with the model's majority answer. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, when SICA errs, 83.3\% of constraints support the incorrect answer---bias ratio $4.97\times$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias})---and 94\% of errors overlap with SC. A shuffle control reveals this bias is \emph{structural}: randomizing constraint--answer associations while preserving per-trace counts yields $\text{BR}_\text{null} \approx \text{BR}_\text{obs}$ ($p > 0.7$), indicating that BR reflects the trace-count distribution rather than extraction-specific error. Z3 rejects only 3.4\% of constraints, and MAX-SAT produces answers identical to count-only aggregation on 94.4\% of problems on Mistral-7B FOLIO (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}), confirming that the formal verification layer is vacuous (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}): 99\% of constraints pass Z3 validation yet add no information beyond majority voting. This vacuity is $\alpha$-insensitive: agreement $\geq$99\% across $\alpha \in \{0.1, \ldots, 0.9\}$ for both high- and moderate-BR conditions (Table~\ref{tab:alpha-ablation}).

\looseness=-1 This pattern generalizes: $4.97\times$ (Mistral), $3.93\times$ (Qwen-14B), $2.88\times$ (LLaMA), all $> 1.0$ with 95\% bootstrap CIs (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}). Cross-model extraction confirms SCB universality across nine models from 7B to frontier (BR $1.66$--$4.97\times$; Table~\ref{tab:frontier-scb}). BR $> 1$ further holds across all eight cells of a $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix on FOLIO and ProofWriter (range $1.94$--$2.70$; Appendix~\ref{app:2x2}). Constraints use trace-specific variables (56.4\% zero cross-answer overlap), collapsing aggregation to majority voting.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\caption{Symbolic confirmation bias across nine models on FOLIO ($n = 204$). BR: mean per-problem bias ratio. $\kappa_{\text{proc}}$: process-level cosine similarity. Ext.: $^a$self-extraction; $^b$cross-model (Mistral-7B extracts FOL from target traces). $^*$Interim ($n = 95/204$). All models exhibit $\text{BR} > 1$; frontier models show relatively lower BR ($1.66$--$2.79\times$ for o3/Gemini), suggesting SCB severity may decrease with capability, though confounded by extraction method ($^a$ vs.\ $^b$).}
\label{tab:frontier-scb}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Model & Ext. & SC (\%) & $\Delta$ (pp) & BR & $\kappa_{\text{proc}}$ \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & $^a$ & 55.88 & $+$3.43 & 4.97 & 0.690 \\
LLaMA-8B & $^a$ & \textrm{---} & \textrm{---} & 2.88 & \textrm{---} \\
Qwen-14B & $^a$ & 75.49 & $+$0.25 & 3.93 & \textrm{---} \\
\addlinespace
R1-Distill-8B & $^b$ & 74.51 & $+$2.45 & 2.92 & 0.248 \\
gpt-4o & $^a$ & 73.91 & $-$1.86 & 2.94 & 0.790 \\
gpt-4.1 & $^b$ & 82.84 & $-$1.47 & 2.70 & 0.792 \\
gpt-5.5 & $^b$ & 84.31 & $+$0.49 & 3.48 & 0.563 \\
o3 & $^b$ & 82.65 & 0.00 & 1.66 & 0.596 \\
Gemini-2.5-Pro$^*$ & $^b$ & 87.37 & $-$1.05 & 2.79 & 0.790 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\looseness=-1 Two controls rule out answer leakage. BR is uniform across early, mid, and late reasoning steps (Fisher $p = 0.71$); high-consensus errors amplify to $\text{BR} = 18.8\times$ versus $2.7\times$ for low-consensus ($p < 0.001$). Removing the answer sentence from traces before extraction yields no BR decrease (Wilcoxon $p \geq 0.12$) despite significant accuracy loss (McNemar $p < 0.013$). SCB reflects a distributional prior embedded throughout reasoning, not answer contamination.

\subsection{Process-Level Independence}
\label{sec:process-kappa}

\looseness=-1 We quantify reasoning-process overlap via ProcessSim, the mean pairwise cosine similarity of TF-IDF trace vectors (Eq.~\ref{eq:process-sim}, Appendix~\ref{app:process-gradient}). ProcessSim captures lexical overlap; deeper structure measures may reveal additional patterns, so we treat it as supporting rather than primary evidence. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, mean ProcessSim $= 0.69$, far exceeding answer-level $\kappa = 0.46$: traces share reasoning processes even when answers diverge. The gap generalizes across four families (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}), from $0.31$ (LLaMA) to $0.02$ (Qwen-14B). Under cross-model extraction, SICA gain decreases monotonically with ProcessSim ($\rho = -1.0$, $p = 0.008$; Table~\ref{tab:process-gradient}), modulated by self-bias severity.


\subsection{Conditional Correction Ratio}
\label{sec:cil}

\looseness=-1 The Conditional Correction Ratio (CCR; definition and bootstrap analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:cil}) confirms the pattern: all cross-architecture NLI pairs on ProofWriter yield $\text{CCR} > 0$ (verifier preferentially corrects generator errors), while same-model SICA yields $\text{CCR} < 0$ (shared bias). CCR correlates with combo gain ($\rho = 0.84$, $p = 0.014$; Appendix~\ref{app:cil}), linking SCB ($\text{CCR} < 0$), cross-architecture escape ($\text{CCR} > 0$), and the aggregation ceiling ($\text{CCR} \to 0$ as generator capability rises).

\subsection{Remediation Analysis}
\label{sec:diagnostics}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

\looseness=-1 All 27 training-free remediation strategies spanning four categories produce neutral-to-negative results (Figure~\ref{fig:interventions-summary}; Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}). Debate achieves $\kappa = 0.15$ but collapses accuracy ($-18.6$\,pp); all others cluster at $\kappa \approx 0.40$--$0.47$, a same-model ceiling (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}). No individual strategy achieves significance after correction (Appendix~\ref{app:intervention-individual}). Oracle controls show the extraction-quality ceiling is $+29.90$\,pp---trained extractors are the most promising future direction.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_interventions_compact.pdf}
\caption{Twenty-three of 27 training-free strategies across four categories (four additional strategies tested but omitted for clarity). All produce neutral-to-negative $\Delta$ vs.\ SC. Grey band: $\pm 1$\,pp noise zone.}
\label{fig:interventions-summary}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Symbolic Mirage Hypothesis: verification.}
\looseness=-1 The three predictions of \S\ref{sec:mirage} are confirmed. (1)~BR $> 1$ in all tested conditions---three self-extraction models, six cross-model pairs, and all eight cells of a $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). (2)~No same-model intervention reduces BR below 1.0; 27 strategies fail (\S\ref{sec:remediation}). (3)~Cross-architecture NLI escapes the mirage when the verifier exceeds the generator (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}).

